/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-136811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+(-2)=48.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-136811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-136811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-136811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "36"] ["math" "38"] ["math" "40"] ["math" "46"] ["math" "48"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-136811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "48"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-136822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}40 & -20 & -60 \\\\ -20 & 10 & a \\\\ c & 30 & 90\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ b \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-136822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}40 & 20 & -60 \\\\ -20 & 10 & 30 \\\\ -60 & 30 & 90\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=30+5+(-60)=-25.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-136822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-136822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-136822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-15"] ["math" "-25"] ["math" "-35"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "15"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-136822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-25"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 21 \\\\ 21 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}21 & 21 \\\\ 21 & 21\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=21+21+6=48.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-41912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "21"] ["math" "30"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "42"] ["math" "48"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "48"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=5 \\\\ 14{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}245 & 70 & a \\\\ 70 & 20 & 10 \\\\ c & 10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}35 \\\\ b \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 14 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 14 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}245 & 70 & 35 \\\\ 70 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 35 & 10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}35 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=35+10+35=80.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-41909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "100"] ["math" "58"] ["math" "72"] ["math" "75"] ["math" "80"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "80"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $15a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}40 & -20 \\\\ -20 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -10 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n15a+\\frac{b}{c}=15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-41904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first row, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot 0+(-1)=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-137034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-4(\\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2+{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-40943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a-3b-6c?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(1/6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(1/2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable.  \n\nFrom the third row, we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=1,\n$$\n\nand from the first row we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a-3b-6c=2(\\frac{1}{2})-3(1)-6(0)=-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-40950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -18 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n3a+\\frac{b}{c}=3\\cdot \\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-40921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-40921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41919" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $30a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41919" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 30\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}30 & -30 & 1 \\\\ -30 & 30 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}30 & -30 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{30}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n30a+\\frac{b}{c}=30\\cdot \\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{1}{1}=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41919" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-41919" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41919" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-41919" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a-\\frac{3b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 36\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 18 & 36 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-\\frac{3b}{c}=-1-\\frac{3(-2)}{1}=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-137096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-137061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $7a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 \\\\ -28 & 56\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & -4 \\\\ -28 & 56 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{7}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{7}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n7a+\\frac{b}{c}=7\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{7})+\\frac{2}{1}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8a752131fcaa791fa1d1a431070e98bf3aff74b53bf4dd95cef5eba43a43ca1"} {"question" "q-137119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $4a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ -14 & 14 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n4a+\\frac{b}{c}=4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{1}=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/state.json" "file_sha256" "f19cd6c504b26047c6e1f0bd7d5bd81e01500ec9105516223f9b130e5d3a6e87"} {"question" "q-137095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture"