trix form as \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/c2daef6c8fee0dd47562395ec76a2912eaea98e3a873d6319553ed281745d210.png)\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & -2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}80 & -60 & -20 \\\\ -60 & 45 & 15 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 5\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}32 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=(-20)-(-24)+(-20) = -16.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/example-5963.json" "file_sha256" "24391d19964b4c31ae27de24145121454feaed365c76bc9436866ea12be7a1f2"} {"question" "e-5590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/98b9eaa366bb0c1dcf899eb231b34e9704335a294d4b69c6af10197eb14929ce.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/example-5590.json" "file_sha256" "91c1a4b7dce39c0adf9fb9df3f648db20a6309f6bed994157159ed1d03f2a1fb"} {"question" "e-5590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{2}{3}+3x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/b36e5a28c59c1ab0be811fb94fec4dc018b6687229f4acb3ef11a745fdeb6631.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/example-5590.json" "file_sha256" "91c1a4b7dce39c0adf9fb9df3f648db20a6309f6bed994157159ed1d03f2a1fb"} {"question" "e-5591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A \\mathbf x = \\mathbf b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/example-5591.json" "file_sha256" "78973c8c0d6cc1bc3f26871611417c73a092320f9752fda5be5dc455abd9d8be"} {"question" "e-5591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/8803a165894746f62c8d27530eebd791d03cfc16ee8da0dd3e176c30a400ee4a.png)\n\nThe least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 144\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ -36 & 144 & -12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac{1}{3}+ 4x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/1f35d807d723ae25214ce1cdd9fdb42c5111f6a0495a5f453e8a04b415863466.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/example-5591.json" "file_sha256" "78973c8c0d6cc1bc3f26871611417c73a092320f9752fda5be5dc455abd9d8be"}] "usage" {"q-41912" [] "e-5590" [] "e-5963" [] "q-41919" [] "q-41909" [] "q-40943" [] "e-5591" []} "basis" 2533 "retractions" [[17592186134298 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax = b$ is given below, what is the value of $a + b + c$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & 21 \\\\ 21 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186134298 :question/answer-fields 17592186132230] [17592186134297 :question/problem "${7x_{1} + 2x_{2} + x_{3} = 5; 14x_{1} + 4x_{2} + 2x_{3} = 0\\begin{bmatrix}245 & 70 & a \\\\ 70 & 20 & 10 \\\\ c & 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}35 \\\\ b \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a + b + c$?"] [17592186134258 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax = b$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix} + x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x_{3} ∈ R$\nwhat is the value of $a + 2b + c$?"] [17592186134299 :question/problem "${x_{1} - x_{2} = 1; 5x_{1} - 5x_{2} = 0; 2x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 0$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2} ∈ R$\n\nwhat is the value of $30a + \\frac{b}{c}$?"] [17592186133557 :question/problem "${4x_{1} - 3x_{2} - x_{3} = 4; 8x_{1} - 6x_{2} - 2x_{3} = 2$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c$?"] [17592186133557 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax &= A^{T}b \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}80 & -60 & -20 \\\\ -60 & 45 & 15 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}32 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=(-20)-(-24)+(-20) = -16\n$$"] [17592186133531 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$."] [17592186133531 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 12\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{2}{3}+3x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} + 3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186133532 :question/problem "${-x_{1} + 4x_{2} = 1; 2x_{1} - 8x_{2} = 1; - 2x_{1} + 8x_{2} =-1$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A \\mathbf x = \\mathbf b$."] [17592186133532 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 144\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ -36 & 144 & -12\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + 4R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac{1}{3}+ 4x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} + 4x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131991 17592186131992 17592186131993 17592186131994 17592186131995 17592186132225 17592186132226 17592186132227 17592186132228 17592186132229 17592186132231 17592186132232 17592186132233 17592186132234 17592186132235 17592186132237 17592186132238 17592186132239 17592186132240 17592186132241] "immutable_fields" [[17592186131990 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131990 :answer-field/id] [17592186131990 :answer-field/key] [17592186131990 :answer-field/type] [17592186132224 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132224 :answer-field/id] [17592186132224 :answer-field/key] [17592186132224 :answer-field/type] [17592186132230 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132230 :answer-field/id] [17592186132230 :answer-field/key] [17592186132230 :answer-field/type] [17592186132236 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132236 :answer-field/id] [17592186132236 :answer-field/key] [17592186132236 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
